  Severity ranking for the final list:

  1. sync_cluster.sh: headers (and TdfMzCalibration.h) overwritten without backup and NOT restored on unit-test/build failure — source/header drift on the node, exactly the failure mode the script exists to prevent; "source restored" message is now false for headers. (Deploy, high.)

  2. In-tree (non-CMake) builds don't get -ffp-contract=off, and the contraction-sensitive recovery code in DnoiseRun::frame (r*corr+0.5, mzToTof) sits in spextractor.cpp outside DnoiseMs1.h's pragma/volatile guards; the node unit test compiles only the header, so it cannot detect contraction in the recovery. Boundary points flip → false refusals (fail-closed) — or, before the first refusal, nothing detects it. Fix: apply the same flag/pragma to the tool TU in the OpenMS topp CMake, or move recovery arithmetic into guarded inline functions in the header, and extend the unit test to pin recovery on pinned doubles.

  3. No automated test of point recovery at all: scanOf/mzToTof/intensity-inversion live in spextractor.cpp; test_dnoise_ms1.cpp pins only the port's filter/gate; e2e can't build .d. The only evidence is two manual digests. A regression (e.g., OpenMS changing the loader's float cast) compiles green and is caught only on the cluster — at run time, hours in, or as a refusal. Fix: factor recovery into a testable header function + golden vectors from D/TNBC (mz, im, intensity, frame → scan, tof, raw), or add an e2e mini-.d fixture (sqlite + blobs are constructible in python).

  4. Recovery assumes opentims computes raw*(100/at) with the correction factor precomputed from atof(sqlite text); if opentims actually evaluates (raw*100)/at in one expression or reads AccumulationTime as a REAL column (sqlite3_column_double), the recovered corr differs by ~1 ulp and boundary points (corrected value at k-0.5 exactly) yield hits==0 → refusal on legitimate data. Since "bit-identity needs the raw one", a wrong-but-unique hit is also conceivable. Verified only on two files; the tolerances (1e-6 bins, exact integer match) mean any such drift fails loudly — acceptable, but the assumption should be pinned by a source citation and a test (ties into 3).

  Actually wait — can a wrong-but-unique hit happen? If corr is 1 ulp off, the forward map r → (uint32)(r*corr+0.5) shifts which r maps to v only at exact boundary .5 ties. The true raw r0 maps to v under opentims arithmetic; under shifted arithmetic, r0 maps to v-1 or v+1, and r0∓1 might map to v → unique hit, wrong raw → silent divergence from dnoise's kept set on those points. This is the dangerous version: NOT fail-closed, silent. The halo ratio uses raw values; a ±1 raw difference flips kept points at 0.15 fraction boundaries — exactly like the "9 of 3.1M" example. So item 4 is potentially silent corruption, not just refusal. Elevate.

  5. Scan-table validation over global MAX(NumScans) for every TimsCalibration row: (a) rows are checked over scan indices no frame uses (extrapolated region of the rational model can be non-monotone), (b) rows referenced by no MS1 frame still must pass — both refuse legitimate runs. Fix: build/check per-row only up to max NumScans over frames referencing that row.

  6. Corrected intensity ≥ 2^24 (or non-integer floats — impossible ≥2^23; or negative) → hits==0 → whole run refused though dnoise would handle the frame fine. Bright-point saturation: opentims uint32 → float32 rounds above 2^24, so inversion is genuinely ambiguous there; refusal is principled, but it means the tool refuses data dnoise itself processes. Document in help/error; consider reading the raw blob (tof index too) instead of inverting when out of range — or at least state the bound.

  7. Help-text memory/wall claims ("26.46 → 22.41 GB", "wall about −15%") were measured on the preprocessor configuration and carried over; the in-tool path adds per-thread Work scratch (~45 B × raw points × threads, ≈3 GB at 100 threads during MS1 batches on D) inside the same MS1-phase peak that already holds the 3.5 GB raw buffer. The shipped default's peak was not measured here (validation section reports only digests/points). Fix: measure the in-tool peak and correct the help text, or drop the numbers.

  8. Fallback reset: ledger rows other than LC_MS1MAP/LC_SLAB charged during the failed stream (compact-store bytes, LC_SCRATCH if any LedCharge leaks past an exception — RAII should cover, verify) and phase_stats_ "LOAD(stream)" entries survive; cstat/ms1_prune/dnoise.total now reset — good. The reset also runs when the stream failed BEFORE any delivery (harmless). One real question: `LedCharge _raw(LC_SCRATCH, raw_b)` in flushMS1_ — if the exception escapes mid-batch, does the dtor un-charge? If LedCharge is add-on-ctor/sub-on-dtor it's fine; verify. Also `ms1Hist_()` counters accumulated from the partial stream are NOT reset → after fallback, [ms1-hist] double-counts the frames delivered before the failure → the histogram log line is wrong (diagnostic only, not output). Check: ms1Hist counters — reset doesn't touch them. Real (minor) miss in the reset. Also consumer's `frames_seen` — scoped, fine.

  9. NaN-m/z spectrum handling in Ms1Prune::frame: sortByPosition() runs BEFORE the !finite bail, so prune-on reorders a NaN-carrying spectrum that prune-off would pass through in picked order; the comment claims such spectra "stay whole", but the band builder's lower_bound over a NaN spectrum lands by length — and the sort may change which peaks precede the NaN vs the unpruned path. Only matters on corrupt/NaN input; if "kept whole" is the intent, skip the sort for non-finite spectra too (sort first into a copy? you need sorted order for the sample — take the sample from the sorted copy but leave the spectrum untouched). Edge case.

